From: mudzy992 <arena-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:50:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(worker): own the heartbeat in the process, and
 re-assert the schedules

`worker.down` opened again with `heartbeatMissing: true` on a stack whose worker
container was up. The periodic heartbeat is a BullMQ schedule, and that design
has two ways to stop writing while the worker keeps running:

* it is registered exactly once, at boot, by `registerRepeatableJob`, which logs
  `integration_worker_heartbeat_schedule_failed` and moves on when Redis refuses
  it - and a Redis restart, flush or failover drops the registration for good.
  Nothing re-asserted it, so the only way back was a worker redeploy;
* its jobs are served by a `concurrency: 1` processor shared with the DLQ sweep,
  so a slow or stalled sweep delays the heartbeat by design.

Either way the key expires and the watchdog sees *absence* - the one signal that
is also what a cold start, an eviction and a failed read look like. The TTL fix
(3600 s) made a dead worker provably stale, but it cannot help when nothing
writes the key at all.

So the worker process now also writes the heartbeat on its own timer (one SET
every 10 s, unref'd, cleared on destroy) - which is what the pre-4.1 setInterval
did, and the plan already notes that two workers writing the same timestamp is
harmless. The key now means "a worker process is up and can reach Redis", while
"the queue machinery runs" stays the job of `ops:snapshot` / `ops.monitor.stale`.
An evicted key is rewritten within 10 s, i.e. long before `openAfter: 2` ticks.

The maintenance schedules are additionally re-asserted every 5 minutes
(`upsertJobScheduler` is idempotent by scheduler id, so this is two Redis calls
per interval and can never double a job): a registration lost at boot or to a
Redis restart heals without a redeploy.

The runbook now says who writes the key, and what to grep for in the worker log.
